cyclotomicField -- cyclotomic field construction
************************************************

  * Usage: 
        cyclotomicField (i)
  * Inputs:
      * i, an integer, the power of the root to be adjoined.
  * Outputs:
      * S, A cyclotomic field with $1^(1/i)$ adjoined

Description
===========

+------------------------------------+
|i1 : S = cyclotomicField (5)        |
|                                    |
|o1 = S                              |
|                                    |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                 |
+------------------------------------+
|i2 : isField S                      |
|                                    |
|o2 = true                           |
+------------------------------------+
|i3 : r = S_0                        |
|                                    |
|o3 = ww                             |
|       5                            |
|                                    |
|o3 : S                              |
+------------------------------------+
|i4 : (r^9, r^10, r^11)              |
|                                    |
|          3     2                   |
|o4 = (- ww  - ww  - ww  - 1, 1, ww )|
|          5     5     5           5 |
|                                    |
|o4 : Sequence                       |
+------------------------------------+
|i5 : T = S[x,y]                     |
|                                    |
|o5 = T                              |
|                                    |
|o5 : PolynomialRing                 |
+------------------------------------+
|i6 : I = ideal (x-r)                |
|                                    |
|o6 = ideal(x - ww )                 |
|                 5                  |
|                                    |
|o6 : Ideal of T                     |
+------------------------------------+
|i7 : dim I                          |
|                                    |
|o7 = 1                              |
+------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

Strange things can happen with the reduction of the coefficients. In M2 v <=
1.2 dimension is off by one.

